    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 281120\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n420 AM PDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Last day of dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert \n  Friday before the start of a cooling trend. \n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region through \n  Saturday, with gusty winds and heavy rain being the greatest \n  threat.\n\n* Gusty daily afternoon winds possible Saturday through mid-week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through next Thursday.\n\nAn anomalous ridge of high pressure will remain situated just south \nof the Four Corners region through Friday, allowing for one more day \nof extreme temperatures. Highs on Friday are expected to be at least \n8-10 degrees above normal, putting peak temperatures within a few \ndegrees of record highs. A trough approaching the West Coast over \nthe weekend is expected to push the high center east and bring some \nlowering heights to the region. High temperatures on Saturday are \nexpected to drop about 4-6 degrees from Friday, with additional \ncooling on Sunday. At this point, it does not look like the Extreme \nHeat Warning will need to be extended past Friday. As the trough \ncontinues to move inland, high temperatures are forecast to actually \ndrop to around normal to slightly below normal by early next week.\n\nWe will see a modest increase in PWATs Friday as moisture gets \npulled northward in response to the incoming trough, with the 1.00-\ninch line extending across Mohave, Clark, San Bernardino, and \nsouthern Lincoln counties. One thing that hampered storm development \non Thursday was the overall cloud cover along with modest warming \naloft. This generally kept things a bit too stable for thunderstorms \nto develop outside of portions of Lincoln and Mohave counties, but \neven those were generally weak. Going into Friday, the latest \nforecast soundings are showing some slight cooling aloft along with \nbetter CAPE values Friday afternoon. Although the instability is \ngenerally weak, the additional cooling, slightly higher CAPE, and \npossibly more sunshine could lead to a bit more shower and \nthunderstorm coverage. Brief heavy rain and gusty winds will be the \nprimary threat, but with winds aloft forecast to be around 20-30 \nknots, flash flooding is less of a threatthough still possible. \nSome drier air will begin to work into the western CWA Saturday, \npretty much bringing an end to thunderstorm chances; however, \nmoisture and weak instability will remain over the eastern half, \nallowing for at least isolated thunderstorms. Again, the greatest \nthreats will generally be gusty winds and brief heavy rain. By \nSunday and early next week, only the far eastern areas of Mohave \nCounty will have a chance of storms as the drier air continues to \npush east through the forecast area.\n\nWith the incoming trough, we will see increasing wind speeds across \nthe region by late weekend through midweek. The strongest winds will \nlikely occur across the favored areas of Lincoln, Clark, Mohave, and \nSan Bernardino counties, where breezy to windy conditions are \nexpected. Widespread daily afternoon southwest gusts of 30-40 mph \nare possible, but at this time, Wind Advisories are not expected, as \nany wind gusts over 40 mph will generally be isolated.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nand variable winds will pick up out of the south later this \nmorning with 10 to 15 knot sustained winds and 15 to 25 knot wind \ngusts. By early afternoon winds will have veered to the southwest \nand storms will have begun to pop up in the higher terrain \nsurrounding the Las Vegas Valley. Low confidence as to whether or \nnot these storms will hold together long enough once they move off\nthe terrain to result in rain, thunderstorms, or CIGS AOB 10kft \nat the terminal. However, even if the storms do not make it to the\nterminal, strong outflow winds from distant storms will be \npossible. Whatever showers and thunderstorms does develop will \ndiminish after sunset, with wind gusts dropping off and winds \ncontinuing to favor a southwesterly direction through the \novernight hours. Temperatures will exceed 100F 17Z-04Z today. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds will follow \ntypical daily directional trends with speeds of 12 knots or less \nthrough late morning/early afternoon when southerly winds will \npick across the region with 15 to 25 knot wind gusts through the \nafternoon.. The exception to this will be the western Mojave \nDesert, including KDAG, where winds will favor a westerly \ndirection through the TAF period. Convection will develop across \nmuch of the region this afternoon, with gusty erratic winds, \nlightning, rain, and CIGs below 10kft are possible with any \nstorms that develop. Winds and convection will wane after sunset \nearly this evening.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           FRI, AUG 28 \n              Record(Yr) \n\nLas Vegas     110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2017)\nNeedles       116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(2017)\nKingman       107(2009)\nDesert Rock   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     86(2019)* \nBishop        62(1970) \nNeedles       94(2017) \nDaggett       82(2017)* \nKingman       75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley 100(2019)* \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/CLIMATE...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
